00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.320
First, let’s start things off by examining&nbsp;
the general story structure of the MCU.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.360
Thanks for that, Kang. See? This guy&nbsp;
knows what he’s talking about. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.800
I mean absolutely no disrespect to Stan Lee stans&nbsp;
by this, but the MCU runs on some pretty tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.480
and true story formulas. At this point, I think&nbsp;
it’s pretty fair to say that we’re all at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.720
vaguely familiar with the narrative beats of&nbsp;
the iconic Hero’s Journey: A call to adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.400
find a mentor, death and rebirth that&nbsp;
results in some sort of a transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.400
atonement and then returning back to the&nbsp;
beginning changed as a result. Classic stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.280
been done to death. But, exactly like that,&nbsp;
overarching end bosses hit key beats in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.280
stories as well. You can certainly see the pattern&nbsp;
in self-contained stories, but it’s especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.920
apparent when you start looking at memorable&nbsp;
big baddies of epic adventure franchises. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.320
the big villain appears in a film technique that&nbsp;
Steven Spielberg likes to call “the fraction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.080
introduction”. This is where the audience is&nbsp;
teased about the mystery surrounding this new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.040
character. For Thanos, this fraction tease came&nbsp;
as his cameo at the end of the first Avengers film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.480
pegging him as the true mastermind behind Loki’s&nbsp;
Chitauri invasion. Harry Potter had evil Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.640
Voldemort appearing as just a face on the back of&nbsp;
professor Quirrel's head at the end of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.880
movie. Showing the big boss as the motivating&nbsp;
force for a lesser antagonist is a common trope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.680
that’s used to express this particular plot point.&nbsp;
Think of Firelord Ozai’s silhouette ordering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.320
Zuko to hunt down the Avatar, or Grand Moff&nbsp;
Tarkin name dropping the Emperor in a New Hope

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.880
Implying that even Darth Vader answers&nbsp;
to someone higher up on the food chain.&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.200
As a plot moves into Act 2, we’re given a taste of&nbsp;
the villain’s true power. It’s common for writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.440
to give us a hint of this through the use of&nbsp;
specific story beats known as “pinch points.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.640
These “pinch points” are moments that amp&nbsp;
up the narrative tension by putting our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.360
heroes in a jam through increasing doses of the&nbsp;
villain’s intensifying presence. Traditionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.760
there are two major pinch points that happen on&nbsp;
opposite ends of a story’s second act. The first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.800
is usually early in act 2 and is just a simple&nbsp;
reminder of the villain’s strength. The second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.280
foreshadows the biggest complication that the&nbsp;
villain will throw at our heroes. Oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.320
an important character gets killed in the process.&nbsp;
In Harry Potter, the first key pinch showcasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.280
the threat of Voldemort is the death of Cedric&nbsp;
Diggory in Goblet of Fire. The second pinch point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.920
comes when Dumbledore himself is killed off in&nbsp;
movie 6, right before the final push towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.640
the end battle. For the Infinity Saga, Thanos’&nbsp;
first pinch point comes when he enlists Ronan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.280
the Accuser to get the Power Stone in Guardians of&nbsp;
the Galaxy. Through Ronan, we are given a glimpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.440
of the destruction that’ll spread throughout the&nbsp;
universe as Thanos completes his rock collection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.040
The second pinch point is at the start of Infinity&nbsp;
War when Thanos decimates the Asgardians, boxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.000
the previously unbeatable Hulk into retirement,&nbsp;
and drives home that even the slippery Loki can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.560
escape his inevitability. Loki’s death is the&nbsp;
lynch pin of the pinch point; without it, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.200
stakes wouldn’t be high enough for the audience&nbsp;
to care. In one scene, Thanos decimates what have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.160
otherwise been indestructible heroes, giving&nbsp;
us a sense of the real threat that he poses.&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.480
Which brings us then, to their moment of&nbsp;
victory. After years of scheming in the shadows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.040
the villain finally achieves their goal. When it&nbsp;
comes to sprawling franchise-level narratives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.200
this moment often happens in the second-to-last&nbsp;
installment, leaving us on a cliffhanger so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.160
both fans and heroes have to sit through their&nbsp;
darkest hour for years waiting for resolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.880
Just think about the king of this trope: Star&nbsp;
Wars: The Empire Strikes Back where Han ends the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.080
movie as a big ol’ ice cube and Luke loses a hand.&nbsp;
For the Infinity Saga, this moment is when Thanos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:29.040
bests Earth’s mightiest heroes and dusts half the&nbsp;
universe. This structure achieves two purposes:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.560
First it underlines the strength of the threat.&nbsp;
But more importantly, it turns our heroes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.880
underdogs. It’s not certain whether or not&nbsp;
they’ll be able to prevail. The next installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.040
then, means that the heroes have to win back&nbsp;
every inch of the world that they wish to fix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.640
ultimately making the climactic&nbsp;
battle feel much more earned.&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.680
So, those are your “Lessons from the Screenplay”.&nbsp;
And, according to this time-tested story formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.160
we can look at Kang’s confirmed appearances&nbsp;
in the MCU to deduce how he might be similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.040
utilized across multiple movies in order to&nbsp;
truly rise to the role of the next big bad.&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.040
Right now, we’ve seen Kang’s first teaser&nbsp;
moment in the season 1 Loki finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.040
and boy was that a journey on its own. Throughout&nbsp;
the season, we see timelines getting deleted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.480
Infinity Stones turned into meaningless rocks, and&nbsp;
a hangry cloud monster that devours all matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:20.480
This leads to the Citadel at the End of Time,&nbsp;
a big scary castle with big dramatic doors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.960
massive tension-building elevators. With the&nbsp;
entire series building to this moment, Loki has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.560
reached the one pulling the strings of the whole&nbsp;
operation. The elevator doors open and...KANG.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.760
It’s his Thanos first reveal moment. And this&nbsp;
isn’t even a particularly violent Kang either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.240
Sure he’s created a Big Brother organization&nbsp;
run by puppets that prunes entire timelines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.920
but he tells us that all of this is to keep his&nbsp;
other, more violent selves at bay selves at bay

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.320
With just one timeline, there’s just one Kang,&nbsp;
and he warns that his death is gonna unleash a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.480
Pandora’s box of crueler variants. So of course&nbsp;
he dies, only to have everything start to unravel.&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.720
Looking more broadly, right now, the multiverse&nbsp;
is in flux with movies like No Way Home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.400
and Multiverse of Madness. While Kang isn’t&nbsp;
connected to either of those events…yet…those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:13.440
movies are already priming us for Kang’s impending&nbsp;
multiversal war. We are learning the rules and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.720
dangers that accompany wild multiversal collisions&nbsp;
just like the Infinity Saga introduced us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.800
one by one, to the powers and dangers of&nbsp;
the individual stones via movies that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.160
completely disconnected from Thanos. The Reality&nbsp;
Stone in Thor 2, The Time Stone in Doctor Strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.080
the tesseract in Avengers, the power stone&nbsp;
in Guardians. That way when everything comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.280
together with Kang, all the rule setting and world&nbsp;
building has already been done in previous movies.&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.720
All of this means that, if we consult&nbsp;
the handy-dandy story cheat sheet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.800
the next time we’ll be interacting with Kang will&nbsp;
be the “pinch points.” And, hey, wouldn’t you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.760
it but we have two projects next year to have&nbsp;
that pair of pinch points. One of these has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.960
be Loki Season 2, due out in mid-2023. This one’s&nbsp;
obvious because, at the end of Season 1 of Loki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.840
we see giant Kang statues in the new layout of&nbsp;
the rebooted TVA. It’s safe to say that Kang is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:03.440
going to play a HUGE role in Loki’s return,&nbsp;
which means there’ll be plenty of time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:07.680
get one of those Pinches in. But the other pinch&nbsp;
point is going to come even sooner than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:14.320
with Quantumania’s release in February of 2023.&nbsp;
And that one is going to be the DEATH PINCH!&nbsp;

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:16.320
This is wild
